    <p><strong>Lane reading.</strong> Unison (identity is a hash of structure), egg (programs as equivalence classes), interaction nets (computation as local rewriting) and proof assistants (correctness as an object) each hold one piece of what the Laws of Coding state together: one canonical Form, exact replay, projection to targets, and checkable acceptance. Elliott's denotational design and the propagator account are earlier statements of the same demand: meaning defines the implementation, and computation adds information rather than only emitting text. They are independent convergence, each from a different starting point. Chollet's measure says the thing to count is how efficiently a system acquires a skill, which is why a comparison of proposers has to hold energy fixed. That comparison is H15. It has not been run.</p>

    <h2 id="4-why-now-the-case-for-closed-form-deterministic-ai-proposed-code">4. Why now: the case for closed-form, deterministic, AI-proposed code</h2>
    <p><strong>This section is opinion, built from the history above and from the three lanes in Section 3. It is a case to be tested, not a result.</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Checkers are cheap and trustworthy where the earlier attempts lacked them.</strong> Using Csmith, a random program generator, the authors found that CompCert was "the only compiler we have tested for which Csmith cannot find wrong-code errors" after "about six CPU-years" of trying (Yang et al., 2011, Sec. 3.1, PDF p. 6). Machine-checked rules can carry the trust that a generator cannot. CompCert itself uses this pattern: its register allocator runs an unverified graph-coloring heuristic and checks each result with a verifier proved correct in Coq, because graph coloring is "easier to validate a posteriori than to directly prove correct" (Leroy, 2009, Sec. 4.2, PDF p. 7).</li>
      <li><strong>Deterministic semantics answer the nondeterminism problem.</strong> If the generator is allowed to vary but the accepted artifact is checked and replayed exactly, run-to-run variation in the model stops mattering for the result.</li>
      <li><strong>Verifier-in-the-loop evidence exists.</strong> AlphaCode's behavioral filtering, Codex-S's oracle test selection (77.5% versus 44.5% for log-probability ranking), Clover, AutoVerus and AlphaProof all show large gains when a check selects among proposals. Section 3 records the same shape in later systems: a model proposes, and an evaluator, a proof kernel or a type system decides.</li>
      <li><strong>Learned predictors now work in latent space.</strong> I-JEPA and V-JEPA 2 show that predicting a summary of the target, rather than every detail, can work well for those tasks: V-JEPA 2 had higher success on 4 of 5 robot tasks (tied on one) and planned about 15x faster than a generative video model in its comparison (Table 3, PDF p. 15). In a program, details such as identifier spelling, formatting and target language can be filled in deterministically after the structure is chosen. Section 5 states the premise and where it stops once Forms and the Laws of Coding exist.</li>
      <li><strong>Token-by-token generation is expensive.</strong> LLaMA 65B, sharded across 8 to 32 V100 GPUs with a 512-token output limit, used "about 3-4 Joules for a output token", counting GPU energy only (Samsi et al., 2023, Secs. III-C and IV-C, PDF pp. 4-6). In a study of 88 models, text-generation models averaged 0.047 kWh (kilowatt-hours) per 1,000 queries, each query generating only 10 new tokens (Table 2), and "multi-purpose, generative architectures are orders of magnitude more expensive than task-specific systems", partly because a generator chooses among "an entire vocabulary" (Luccioni et al., 2024, abstract, PDF p. 1; Sec. 4.1 and Table 2, PDF p. 6; Sec. 4.2.1, PDF p. 8).</li>
      <li><strong>Illustrative arithmetic only, mixing sources.</strong> At 3-4 joules per token (Samsi et al.), a 300-token function costs about 0.9-1.2 kJ (kilojoules) per sample; at the 28.8% one-sample pass rate of Codex-12B (Chen et al.) that is roughly 3-4 kJ per test-passing function before counting test execution. These numbers come from different models and hardware. A proper test measures both on the same machine.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>What could still fail</h3>
    
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Expressiveness ceiling (H6).</strong> If real programs need effects or nondeterminism the Form cannot state, the "all code" premise fails.</li>
      <li><strong>Intent may be the bottleneck.</strong> A smaller output space does not fix underspecified intent. Signal: H1 and H2 hold while H3 fails.</li>
      <li><strong>Spec gaming.</strong> The checker proves a Form is well-formed and meets stated properties, not that it matches what the user meant. Benchmark tasks carry behavioral traces checked by replay; the residual risk is labeled, not solved.</li>
      <li><strong>Corpus scarcity.</strong> Signal: H3 flat as the corpus grows.</li>
      <li><strong>Collapse.</strong> JEPAs need anti-collapse measures, such as the EMA (exponential moving average) target encoder of I-JEPA and V-JEPA 2; LeCun (2022) proposes VICReg-style information maximization instead (Sec. 4.5). A collapsed Form embedding makes H5 fail. Section 5 states this limit beside the others.</li>
      <li><strong>Measurement error.</strong> Rootless counters (macmon, RAPL) cover package or SoC, not wall power; both arms use the same meter.</li>
      <li><strong>Baseline drift.</strong> The quoted figures come from 2021-2025 systems; a fair test re-measures a current baseline on the same hardware.</li>
    </ul>

    

    <h2 id="5-jepa-as-premise">5. JEPA as premise, and where it stops</h2>
    <p>The premise stays. A JEPA predicts in an embedding space, not in the space of the raw input. The thesis cites LeCun's 2022 statement of that idea. V-JEPA 2 reports 62 hours of robot video and zero-shot pick-and-place success from 65% to 80% [B1]. The action-conditioned predictor, as the proposer section states from the same report, was trained on fewer than 62 hours of unlabeled robot video. LLM-JEPA reports that an embedding-space loss beats a token loss on a natural-language-to-regular-expression task, on GSM8K (a grade-school math set) and on Spider (a text-to-database query set), at 1 billion parameter scale (Huang, LeCun and Balestriero, 2025). For code, this matches the intuition that the useful object is meaning, not tokens.</p>
    <p>Once Forms exist, and once the Laws of Coding fix equivalence and acceptance, that premise is not the whole method. Six limits follow. They are reasons to widen the proposer, not reasons to drop the premise.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>An approximate geometry, where an exact one can be computed.</strong> JEPA learns an approximate latent geometry. The Laws of Coding give an exact one: the canonical Form and the equivalences the checker already decides. Learning those by self-supervision spends data on a map that can be computed. H10 is the test of whether learning on the quotient saves data. H10 has not been run.</li>
      <li><strong>Collapse.</strong> Representation collapse, and the need for regularizers against it, are open problems in JEPA training. An exact canonicalizer cannot collapse. It maps each surface program to one Form by a rule, not by a loss.</li>
      <li><strong>No native acceptance.</strong> JEPA has no native decoder and no acceptance step. Code needs a discrete, checkable output. The energy score ranks candidates. It does not decide. The checker decides.</li>
      <li><strong>Points, not a quotient.</strong> Alternate Forms are a quotient. Many surface programs can be one Form, and one Form can be projected to many targets. JEPA embeds points. It does not certify that two points are the same Form. An e-graph does. A hash of structure does. Both are already in Section 3.</li>
      <li><strong>Scale of the evidence.</strong> LLM-JEPA's evidence is at 1 billion to 2 billion parameter scale, on small datasets. No source read for this page shows JEPA training for repository-scale code.</li>
      <li><strong>The cost of learned planning.</strong> V-JEPA 2's model predictive control (MPC) took about 16 seconds per action [B1]. Search over Forms with a cheap exact checker may dominate a learned rollout. That comparison has not been run. This page states no joule figure for it. A joule is a unit of electrical energy. Idle use is subtracted when a meter reading exists. OpenIE explains that unit in the open, and does not fill a number in for a run that has not happened.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Consequence.</strong> JEPA stays as the premise: predict meaning, not text. The proposer slot generalizes to any learned proposer over Forms. The instances named in Section 3 are autoregressive models with execution mid-training (CWM), diffusion code models, reinforcement learning in a formal environment (AlphaProof and the Lean provers), evolutionary search (AlphaEvolve), library learning (DreamCoder and LILO), and constrained decoding. Large Concept Models sit beside the premise in language, not yet in code. The gate is the same for all of them. Section 6 is the taxonomy. Section 7 says what the world is that they propose into.</p>

    <h2 id="6-proposer-slot">6. The proposer slot is architecture-neutral</h2>
    <p>Figure 1 draws one pipeline. The learned box is a slot, not a single architecture. JEPA is the instance in the figure. The gate drawn outside the box does not change if a different proposer is plugged in. The table says, for each kind of proposer read in Section 3, what it learns, what it needs from the Laws of Coding (a canonicalizer, a checker, a cost meter), the failure mode the sources already name, and which hypothesis it would move. "Would move" means the hypothesis the experiment is aimed at. It is not a result. No learned proposer has been trained for this project.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-xs border-collapse [&_td]:border [&_th]:border [&_td]:border-brand-border [&_th]:border-brand-border [&_td]:p-2 [&_th]:p-2 [&_th]:text-left [&_th]:text-brand-white [&_td]:align-top">
      <thead><tr><th>Proposer</th><th>What it learns</th><th>What it needs from the checker</th><th>Known failure mode</th><th>Hypothesis it would move</th></tr></thead>
