    <p>The deterministic ladder in the proposer section runs before any of these. H14 measures that ladder. The learned slot acts on the residual. After the plain arm of run 3, the residual is 40 tasks. H15 is the unfinished comparison of four classes on those 40 tasks at equal energy: JEPA, trace-trained models, diffusion, and evolutionary search. Equal energy means the same electrical energy, idle subtracted when a device reading exists. No joule figure is stated, because the run does not exist.</p>

    <h2 id="7-world-of-code">7. A World of Code</h2>
    <p><strong>Definition.</strong> A World of Code is the state space of Forms, plus the exact transition and the exact acceptance rule. In the notation of the thesis, the state space is <span class="not-prose">S</span>, the transition is the function δ, and acceptance is the checker's verdict on a Form. "World model", for code, means a model of this space. Unlike a physical world model, the physics is known exactly. Part 4 of the thesis says that for a JEPA-style predictor. The same physics bounds every proposer in Section 6. CWM learns an interpreter from traces. V-JEPA 2 learns physics from video. In a Form, the transition is given. There is no irreducible noise in the next state.</p>
    <p><strong>Where learning is removed, and where it remains.</strong> Canonicalization, equivalence and replay are computations. Once the Laws of Coding fix them, a model does not have to learn them. What remains to learn is the map from a stated intent to a Form, guidance of the search, and ranking of whatever the deterministic ladder leaves open. That residual is the 40 tasks of H14 run 3. H15 asks which proposer class closes more of them at equal energy. H15 is open work. It is not a result.</p>
    <p><strong>Evaluation.</strong> A test suite is not the Laws of Coding. PatchDiff reports that 7.8% of patches counted correct fail developer tests, and that resolve rates are inflated by about 6.4 points. The METR trial reports 16 developers, 246 tasks, and a measured completion time 19% higher with early-2025 tools. SWE-Bench Pro, on the board fetched 6 November 2026, still uses fail-to-pass and pass-to-pass tests as the oracle. Multi-SWE-bench widens the set of languages to Java, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go, Rust, C and C++. A November 2026 leaderboard figure for it was not retrieved, and none is stated. Acceptance on this page is the gate, plus the replay hash. A passing test is evidence. It is not acceptance.</p>
    <p><strong>Falsifier of the widened claim.</strong> RJ-F6, stated with the others in Section 8, names the observation that would defeat the Form quotient as a help to proposers. If a token-space model trained with execution traces, of the CWM class, closes the residual of 40 tasks at equal energy and with the same gate as the acceptance rule, then the quotient adds nothing for proposers. That observation would tie to H3 and to H10. It has not been made. Energy in that sentence means joules on a named device, with idle use subtracted. The number is blank until the run exists.</p>

    <h2 id="8-one-concrete-instance-the-reality-code-engine">8. One concrete instance: the Reality Code Engine</h2>
    <p>RCE is one closed program form among possible others; the questions above apply to any language with a small, checkable program shape. In RCE every program is a Form P = (S, E, δ, D, λ):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>S, state:</strong> the typed data the program holds, for example the cursor position and text buffer of an editor.</li>
      <li><strong>E, events:</strong> the inputs it can receive, for example "key pressed" or "file opened".</li>
      <li><strong>δ (delta), transition function:</strong> the rule giving the next state from the current state and an event.</li>
      <li><strong>D, derivations:</strong> the recorded, hash-chained history of steps, which makes every run replayable bit for bit.</li>
      <li><strong>λ (lambda), output:</strong> what the program shows or emits for a given state, for example the visible lines of text.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A checker accepts a Form or names the rule it breaks. The public reference checker replays an accepted Form exactly and emits it as code in other languages (Rust, Python, JavaScript and Scala), and the theorems of the language are proved in Lean 4. The design of RCE's engine, which is to run accepted Forms and place work across hardware, is described in the RCE paper. The full description is on the <a href="/about/rce/">RCE topic page</a> and the public specification at <a href="https://github.com/openIE-dev/rce">github.com/openIE-dev/rce</a>; nothing below depends on having read them.</p>
    <p><strong>RCE-JEPA</strong> is the working name, kept so the thread stays findable. The subject is World Coding. JEPA, a joint embedding predictor, is the premise: predict meaning, not surface text. The proposer slot is any learned proposer over Forms. Checking, proofs, derivation, emission and placement stay with RCE. The aim is the best performance per clock cycle and per joule. <em>Working hypothesis:</em> if all code can be encoded in the Form, all code can be produced by joint embedding or energy-based models over it. The hypotheses below are designed so that the evidence can refute this.</p>

    <h3>Defined quantities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Output space.</strong> Free-form generation over vocabulary V with length L has raw space |V|<sup>L</sup>. Ω<sub>n</sub> (omega) is the set of valid Forms of size n (Form nodes); Ω<sub>n</sub><sup>✓</sup> ⊆ Ω<sub>n</sub> is the subset the checker accepts. Reported as log<sub>2</sub>|Ω<sub>n</sub>| bits, estimated by grammar counting and sampling.</li>
      <li><strong>Description length.</strong> b(P) is the bits to encode Form P canonically; b(code) = tokens × log<sub>2</sub>|V| for the equivalent emitted source. Ratio ρ (rho) = b(code) / b(P).</li>
      <li><strong>Verification cost.</strong> C<sub>check</sub>(P): wall time, cycles and joules for the checker to decide P. Unit tests give C<sub>test</sub>; a passing test is evidence, not proof.</li>
      <li><strong>Acceptance probability.</strong> a = P(checker accepts and task specification satisfied | intent), measured as pass@1 over the benchmark.</li>
      <li><strong>Joules per accepted program.</strong> J<sub>acc</sub> = (E<sub>model</sub> + E<sub>check</sub> + E<sub>retry</sub>) / N<sub>accepted</sub>, summed over a benchmark run, energy measured at the device with idle power subtracted.</li>
      <li><strong>Cycles per accepted program.</strong> K<sub>acc</sub>, the same in CPU (central processing unit) or GPU cycles where counters are readable.</li>
      <li><strong>AUROC</strong> (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve): the probability that a randomly chosen accepted Form gets a lower energy than a randomly chosen one that requires change; 0.5 is chance, 1.0 is perfect.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Argument (opinion, to be tested).</strong> A Form has five typed slots with fixed composition rules. Constraining output to Ω<sub>n</sub> removes most of |V|<sup>L</sup> before the model is consulted, so fewer bits are predicted per program (ρ &gt; 1), the checker gives a definite verdict, accepted or requires change, in place of a probabilistic test, and a requires-change verdict is cheap and structured, naming the rule, so retries are guided rather than blind.</p>
    <h3 id="hypotheses">Falsifiable hypotheses H1 to H15</h3>
    <p>All fifteen hypotheses in numerical order. H1 to H7 test the RCE-JEPA thesis directly, H8 to H10 test the "Laws of Coding" thesis above, H11 to H13 test the proposer, H14 measures the deterministic ladder beneath it, and H15 compares proposer classes on the residual and is unfinished (the ladder is described in the proposer section above; the H3 control is in the proposer section above; the falsifiers RJ-F1 to RJ-F6 follow this table). All measured on the benchmark below, same hardware, idle-subtracted joules. Baseline: an next-token code LLM of matched parameter count generating source in a target language, judged by that language's tests plus the RCE spec where translatable.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-xs border-collapse [&_td]:border [&_th]:border [&_td]:border-brand-border [&_th]:border-brand-border [&_td]:p-2 [&_th]:p-2 [&_th]:text-left [&_th]:text-brand-white">
