      <thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Observation</th><th>What it defeats</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>RJ-F1</td><td>Lift coverage plateaus below 80% on any of the five target languages after the construct list is exhausted</td><td>The claim that all code can be emitted from RCE, and with it the scope of autonomous coding over Forms</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RJ-F2</td><td>Writing σ(I) for the 7 reference applications costs more person-hours than writing their Forms by hand</td><td>The claim that closure removes, rather than moves, the specification cost</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RJ-F3</td><td>H3 fails while the token arm passes at the same gate</td><td>Latent proposal over Form space; the gate still stands, the JEPA half does not</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RJ-F4</td><td>H11 fails and search does not reach accepted Forms within the beam budget</td><td>Energy-guided planning in Form space</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RJ-F5</td><td>The decision head's calibration never beats a frequency table over shape hashes</td><td>The decision-model leg; it adds nothing over promoted-handler statistics</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RJ-F6</td><td>A token-space model trained with execution traces, of the CWM class, closes the residual of 40 tasks from the plain arm of H14 run 3, at equal energy and with the same gate as the acceptance rule</td><td>The claim that the Form quotient adds something for proposers. It ties to H3 and H10. Not observed. H15 is the unfinished comparison</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>

    <p>The thesis is disproved overall if H4 fails (no joule advantage per accepted program) or H6 fails (the Form cannot express the reference apps). Either alone is sufficient.</p>

    
    <h3>What will be delivered</h3>
    <p>Scope, organised as parallel workstreams that meet at the evaluation harness. Every deliverable either produces Forms, produces models that propose Forms, or measures the result.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>W1. Form corpus and dataset card.</strong> Forms paired with intent statements and checker verdicts, from hand-written Forms, reference-app Forms and checker-verified mutations; the card covers provenance, licence, size distribution, negative generation, known gaps and contamination controls; canonical serialization for H1.</li>
      <li><strong>W2. Encoder and predictor.</strong> Intent encoder, EMA Form target encoder, predictor in representation space and energy head E(intent, P), with anti-collapse measures, an embedding-space versus token-space loss ablation, and model cards with measured training energy.</li>
      <li><strong>W3. Checker-constrained decoder.</strong> Grammar-constrained candidate Forms ranked by energy and submitted to the checker, requires-change diagnostics, each naming its rule, fed back under a fixed retry budget; the checker is the sole acceptance authority with no bypass path to the engine.</li>
      <li><strong>W4. Intent-to-Form benchmark.</strong> Unit and composite tasks, the reference apps (game engine, text editor, video player, chat app, browser, terminal) and RCEdb; each task has intent, reference Form, replay traces and an equivalent source-language task for the baseline; held-out split.</li>
      <li><strong>W5. Evaluation harness with energy in joules.</strong> Both arms on identical hardware and tasks; pass@1, pass@k, ρ, C<sub>check</sub>, J<sub>acc</sub>, K<sub>acc</sub>, retries per acceptance, energy-ranking AUROC; rootless counters, idle subtracted, raw logs kept, unavailable counters reported rather than estimated.</li>
      <li><strong>W6. Research article.</strong> This case expanded with results for H1 to H15, including negative results, and including H15 only when a run exists, with figures, tables and reproducibility instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>W7. Open artifacts.</strong> Public: the Form specification, the reference checker and its emitters, benchmark tasks and traces, harness, dataset and model cards, and weights where licences allow. Not public: the engine source and its placement logic. A number is reported only when everything needed to reproduce it is public.</li>
      <li><strong>W8. Demo.</strong> Stated intent to predicted Form to checker verdict (including a shown requires-change verdict) to replay and emitted code in two languages by the reference checker, with an on-screen joule reading.</li>
    </ul>

    
    <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
    <p>Research topic open. The gate, replay, projections and cost counting (integer cost units, with energy estimates labelled <code>est_j</code> and no energy reading) are described in the RCE paper; no number from that paper is used here as evidence. The phased pilot plan runs from the data engine (Phase 0) to convergence (Phase 5), each phase preregistered with exit criteria. Phase 0 has started: version 1 of the Form corpus (W1) and the first measurement of H1 are published (23 October 2026; see the data section of the proposer above), with H1 supported on the hand-written population at a median rho of 9.34 over 185 programs. The data section of the proposer records that version 1 of the corpus gave 9.31 over 173. The published floor is 9.34 over 185. H8 has a first recorded run, preregistered before it: 10,500 seeded event streams over the example programs, traced and hashed on six targets (x86_64, aarch64, s390x big-endian and wasm32; Linux, macOS and Windows) by the repository's replay-hash parity workflow, with 0 mismatching lines against the reference; the run, the targets and the limits are in the experiments repository under <code>rce/bench/determinism/</code>. H7 has a first run on its fuzzed arm, preregistered before it: 1,275 checker-accepted mutants of the Form corpus over two runs were checked against five stated invariants (the hash chain, atomicity of a kept step, determinism of two runs, re-acceptance by the gate, termination under fuel) with 0 violations; the model-generated arm does not exist yet (<code>rce/bench/adversarial/</code>). The proposer, the decision head and the hypotheses H2 to H6 and H9 to H13 remain planned. H15, a comparison of proposer classes on the residual of 40 tasks at equal energy, is unfinished and has no measured result. H14 needs no learned proposer and has three registered runs of the public ladder, <code>rce repair</code> of openIE-dev/rce, each registered before it ran (<code>rce/bench/ladder/exact-proposers/</code>, <code>rce/bench/ladder/real-defects/</code> and <code>rce/bench/ladder/rung-e/</code> in openie-research-experiments) [B30, B31, B32]. Run 3, on run 2's 151 tasks with rung 5 (renames) added, closes 111 of 151 (73.5%) with zero model calls: 83 of 83 renames, 27 of 27 guard negations and 1 of 41 changes from the git history of openIE-dev/rce. By its registered rule, 10% or more of the registered tasks, H14 is supported. The residual a learned proposer must close is the 40 history tasks the ladder leaves open.</p>

